<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pemex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Petróleos Mexicanos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pemex (Petróleos Mexicanos) is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s state oil company, created in 1938 after President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazaro-cardenas">Lázaro Cárdenas</a> expropriated the foreign oil companies. It held a monopoly over oil production for most of its history, and its revenues long financed a large share of the federal budget. Discoveries such as the Cantarell field made it one of the world's largest oil producers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Asphalt and pitch had been worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> since the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, and small quantities of oil were first refined into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerosene">kerosene</a> around 1876 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a>. By the early 20th century subsidiaries of the British Pearson and American Doheny companies were extracting and refining oil in commercial quantities, which drew the attention of the Mexican government, which claimed all mineral rights for the state under its constitution.</p><p>In 1938 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazaro-cardenas">Lázaro Cárdenas</a> sided with oil workers who were striking against the foreign-owned companies for higher pay and social services. On 18 March 1938, citing Article 27 of the Constitution of 1917, he expropriated the resources and facilities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> companies. He asked the nation for the moral and material support needed to face the consequences of a decision that, he said, the government would neither have sought nor desired. Pemex was established by his decree of 7 June 1938. Expropriation was not outright confiscation, since the government promised compensation, but many foreign governments retaliated by closing their markets to Mexican oil until demand for petroleum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> ended the boycott.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pemex became a lasting symbol of Mexican nationalism. In 1979 its Ixtoc 1 exploratory well in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-campeche">Bay of Campeche</a> suffered a blowout that caused one of the largest oil spills in history; the company spent 100 million dollars on the clean-up and avoided most compensation claims by asserting sovereign immunity. On 19 November 1984 a series of explosions at a Pemex liquefied petroleum gas storage facility at San Juan Ixhuatepec, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, killed about 500 to 600 residents and injured up to 7,000 more, and it is counted among the deadliest industrial accidents in history.</p><p>Annual production fell each year between 2004 and 2007. Presidents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-fox">Vicente Fox</a> and Felipe Calderón proposed opening the sector to private investment, and the proposals met opposition in Congress. In 2008 the Mexican Congress created the National Hydrocarbons Commission to increase oversight of the company. Later events included a pipeline explosion at Tlauhuelilpan, Hidalgo, on 18 January 2019, in which at least 137 people died while gathering fuel from an illegal tap. In 2021 Pemex took over the operation of the Zama oil field, which Talos Energy had discovered in 2017, and Talos disputed the decision. The Dos Bocas refinery opened in 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pemex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Revolution produced Article 27 of the Constitution of 1917, which made subsoil resources national property and allowed land redistribution; under Lázaro Cárdenas in the 1930s some 18 million hectares were distributed, largely as communal ejidos, and the foreign oil companies were expropriated in 1938 to form <b>Pemex</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
